<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Party of Labour of Albania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Albanian Communist Party, Communist Party of Albania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Party of Labour of Albania was the ruling communist party of Albania. Founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> in November 1941 as the Albanian Communist Party, with Yugoslav help, it led the Partisan resistance and took power in 1944. Renamed in 1948, it ruled as the only legal party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiz-alia">Ramiz Alia</a> until 1991, when it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-party-of-albania">Socialist Party of Albania</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 1920s Albania was the only Balkan country without a communist party. The first Albanian communists were followers of the clergyman and politician Fan S. Noli, who reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, formed the National Revolutionary Committee and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>. A first Albanian Communist Party was formed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in August 1928, but no unified organisation existed in Albania until 1941. Following the German attack on the Soviet Union in June 1941, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a>, following Comintern directives, sent two Yugoslav delegates, Miladin Popović and Dušan Mugoša, who helped unite the Albanian communist groups. The party was formed on 8 November 1941 by a delegation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korce">Korça</a> branch as its leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The party dominated the National Liberation Movement formed in 1942, which drove out the German occupiers on 29 November 1944. From then Albania was a communist state without a period of coalition government. In the elections of 2 December 1945 voters were offered a single list from the Democratic Front, organised by the party, which received 93.7 per cent of the vote. The party was renamed Party of Labour of Albania in 1948, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> suggested in 1947 that the name should reflect the peasant majority.</p><p>Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> were close until 1953; in 1961 Hoxha broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>, purged pro-Moscow members such as Liri Belishova and Koço Tashko, and aligned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s China. Albania left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> in 1968, and in 1978 Hoxha declared that it would find its own road to socialism.</p><p>Hoxha led the party until his death in 1985, and his successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiz-alia">Ramiz Alia</a>, began gradual reforms. On 11 December 1990 Alia announced that the party had abandoned its monopoly of power. It won the elections of 1991, the first contested ones in decades, and on 13 June 1991 dissolved and refounded itself as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-party-of-albania">Socialist Party of Albania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its ideology was Hoxhaism, an anti-revisionist variant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, and its organisation followed democratic centralism. The 1976 constitution named it the leading political force of state and society. The Party Congress met every five years and elected the Central Committee, which in turn elected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> and a Secretariat. Its daily paper was Zëri i Popullit and its monthly journal Rruga e Partisë.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Party of Labour of Albania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
